1
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:06,980
Quick question. What's the very first thing you remember? Got it? Okay.

2
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,580
Here's the weird part. Whatever it is,

3
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:11,760
you were probably at least three years old.

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:14,550
That means more than a thousand days of your life,

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,120
the days you learned to walk, to talk,

6
00:00:17,230 --> 00:00:24,110
to recognize your mom's face, are just... gone. Deleted.

7
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:25,990
So where did they go?

8
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,650
Why can't you remember being a baby?

9
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:34,120
First, let's kill a myth.

10
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:35,960
Babies are not bad at remembering.

11
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:37,710
In the nineteen eighties,

12
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:44,290
a scientist named Carolyn Rovee-Collier tied a ribbon from a baby's ankle to a mobile hanging over the crib.

13
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:46,170
Kick, and the mobile moves.

14
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:48,280
Babies figured that out fast.

15
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,050
And when she brought them back a week later,

16
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:52,660
three-month-olds still remembered,

17
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,860
and started kicking right away.

18
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:56,990
Older babies remembered for months.

19
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:00,310
So babies do make memories. Tons of them.

20
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:02,930
The problem is, they don't last.

21
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:06,180
Scientists have a name for this.

22
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:08,230
Infantile amnesia.

23
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,600
And there are three big suspects.

24
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:13,640
Suspect number one:

25
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,580
your brain was a construction site.

26
00:01:15,670 --> 00:01:18,820
Deep inside your brain is a part shaped a bit like a seahorse,

27
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:20,630
called the hippocampus.

28
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:24,240
Its job is to take what happens to you and save it as a memory.

29
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:27,720
But in babies, the hippocampus is still being built.

30
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:29,720
It keeps making brand-new neurons,

31
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:35,110
and every new neuron that wires in can scramble the connections that were already there.

32
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:39,230
Imagine writing in a notebook while someone keeps adding and ripping out pages.

33
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,940
In experiments with baby mice,

34
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,990
when scientists slowed down that new-neuron growth,

35
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:45,830
the mice forgot less.

36
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:49,360
Suspect number two: you had no words.

37
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:51,750
Grown-up memories are like files with labels.

38
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:55,610
My tenth birthday. The day I broke my arm.

39
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:58,250
But a baby has no words to label anything.

40
00:01:58,790 --> 00:02:01,730
So even if the memory is in there, later,

41
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:03,570
when you think in words,

42
00:02:03,670 --> 00:02:05,290
you can't search for it.

43
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:09,450
It's like a file saved in a format your computer can't open anymore.

44
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:13,720
Suspect number three: there was no you yet.

45
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:15,940
To remember something happening to you,

46
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:17,780
you need a sense of me.

47
00:02:17,830 --> 00:02:22,120
Most babies don't recognize themselves in a mirror until around eighteen months.

48
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:26,430
Before that, there's no main character to build the story around.

49
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:30,790
Now here's what's really strange.

50
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:34,690
Little kids actually remember their baby years for a while.

51
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:39,930
Psychologist Patricia Bauer recorded three-year-olds talking with their moms about things they'd done.

52
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,590
Years later, she checked what they still remembered. At five,

53
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:46,740
six and seven, the kids remembered most of it.

54
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:49,080
By eight and nine, only about a third.

55
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,400
So the big forgetting doesn't happen when you're a baby.

56
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:56,310
It happens around age seven.

57
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:03,540
But wait. Are those memories really deleted? Maybe not.

58
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:04,800
In twenty eighteen,

59
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:08,280
researchers in Toronto gave baby mice a memory,

60
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:10,230
waited until they had forgotten it,

61
00:03:10,270 --> 00:03:13,940
and then used light to switch on the exact brain cells that stored it.

62
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:15,760
And the mice remembered.

63
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,600
The memory was still there.

64
00:03:17,730 --> 00:03:19,530
They just couldn't find the door.

65
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:21,080
And in twenty twenty-five,

66
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:22,780
a team at Yale scanned babies' brains,

67
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:26,460
and saw the hippocampus saving new memories from about one year old.

68
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:31,080
So your baby memories might be locked away inside you, right now.

69
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:34,690
Which brings us back to your first memory.

70
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,750
In a survey of more than six thousand people,

71
00:03:37,810 --> 00:03:42,320
almost forty percent said they remembered something from age two or younger.

72
00:03:42,690 --> 00:03:46,190
Scientists think most of those are fake. Not lies.

73
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:49,010
Your brain stitched them together from photos,

74
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:51,230
family stories and things you were told,

75
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:53,070
and then filed them as real.

76
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,330
So, why can't you remember being a baby?

77
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:59,100
Your brain was under construction,

78
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:01,030
you had no words, and no me.

79
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:03,100
And around age seven,

80
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:04,960
your brain cleaned house.

81
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:07,660
But the baby you were didn't disappear.

82
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:10,760
Every word you know, every face you trust,

83
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,140
every step you take,

84
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:14,710
you learned in the years you forgot. So,

85
00:04:14,970 --> 00:04:16,650
what's your earliest memory?

86
00:04:16,690 --> 00:04:17,580
Drop it in the comments,

87
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:20,120
and I'll tell you if it's fake.
